# Database providers

Odroe starts with a small typed SQL transport instead of pretending one driver
behaves identically on every runtime.

```dart
abstract interface class SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<T>> query<T>(
    BoundSql statement,
    T Function(SqlRow row) decode,
  );

  Future<SqlWriteResult> execute(BoundSql statement);
}

abstract interface class SqlDatabase implements SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<SqlWriteResult>> atomicWrite(
    List<BoundSql> statements,
  );

  Future<void> close();
}
```

`TransactionalSqlDatabase` is a separate capability. Edge databases that only
support atomic batches do not claim interactive transactions.

## Typed relational queries

```dart
final class Authors extends SqlTable<String> {
  Authors() : super('authors');

  late final id = column<int>('id', sqlInt);
  late final displayName = column<String>('display_name', sqlText);

  @override
  late final projection = SqlProjection.column(displayName);
}

typedef Post = ({int id, int? authorId, String title});

final class Posts extends SqlTable<Post> {
  Posts() : super('posts');

  late final id = column<int>('id', sqlInt);
  late final authorId = column<int?>('author_id', nullable(sqlInt));
  late final title = column<String>('title', sqlText);

  @override
  late final projection = SqlProjection<Post>(
    <SqlSelection<Object?>>[id, authorId, title],
    (row) => (
      id: id.read(row, 0),
      authorId: authorId.read(row, 1),
      title: title.read(row, 2),
    ),
  );
}

final posts = Posts();
final authors = Authors();
const sql = SqlQueries(SqlDialect.postgres);

final post = await sql
    .selectTable(posts, where: posts.id.equals(42))
    .oneOrNull(database);

final selected = await sql
    .selectTable(
      posts,
      where: posts.id.isIn(<int>[42, 44]),
      orderBy: <SqlOrder>[posts.id.ascending],
      limit: 20,
    )
    .all(database);

final authorName = authors.displayName.optional.as('author_name');
final postsWithAuthors = SqlProjection<({String title, String? authorName})>(
  <SqlSelection<Object?>>[posts.title, authorName],
  (row) => (
    title: posts.title.read(row, 0),
    authorName: authorName.read(row, 1),
  ),
);
final rows = await sql.select(
  from: posts,
  joins: <SqlJoin>[
    SqlJoin.left(
      authors,
      on: posts.authorId.equalsColumn(authors.id),
    ),
  ],
  projection: postsWithAuthors,
).all(database);

await sql
    .insertOnConflictDoNothing(
      posts,
      <SqlAssignment>[
        posts.id.set(42),
        posts.title.set('Hello'),
      ],
      target: [posts.id],
    )
    .execute(database);

final inserted = await sql
    .insertMany(posts, <List<SqlAssignment>>[
      <SqlAssignment>[posts.id.set(43), posts.title.set('First')],
      <SqlAssignment>[posts.id.set(44), posts.title.set('Second')],
    ])
    .returning(posts.projection)
    .all(database);
final insertedById = <int, Post>{
  for (final post in inserted) post.id: post,
};

await sql.updateAll(
  posts,
  <SqlUpdateAssignment>[posts.title.set('Archived')],
  confirm: allRows,
).execute(database);

await sql.updateWhere(
  counters,
  <SqlUpdateAssignment>[counters.value.incrementBy(1)],
  where: counters.id.equals(42),
).execute(database);
```

The dialect is explicit. `posts.title.set(42)` is an analyzer error.
`updateAll` and `deleteAll` also require the `allRows` confirmation token.
Joined reads qualify every column automatically. For a `LEFT JOIN`, use the
result-only `.optional` selection to decode a non-null schema column as
nullable; it cannot be used for INSERT or UPDATE. Mutations remain single-table.
In the final update, `counters.value` is a non-null `SqlTableColumn<int>`.

`incrementBy` is available only on a non-null `SqlTableColumn<T extends num>`.
It emits one bound `column = column + ?` assignment, so concurrent writers do
not race through an application-side read-modify-write. Negative values
subtract; numeric zero, including `-0.0`, is rejected before SQL construction.
The column codec still validates and encodes the delta. UPDATE accepts
`SqlUpdateAssignment`, which includes both constant `set` assignments and
numeric increments; INSERT continues to accept only `SqlAssignment`. Duplicate
assignments to one column and assignments owned by another table fail before
database I/O.

`isIn` and `isNotIn` bind each non-null candidate through the receiving
column's codec and snapshot the iterable structure immediately. Empty `isIn`
compiles to a predicate that never matches; empty `isNotIn` throws
`ArgumentError` before it can turn a guarded mutation into a full-table
operation. A Dart `null` candidate is normalized to an explicit `IS NULL` or
`IS NOT NULL` branch instead of being placed inside the membership list. The
column codec remains authoritative for non-null Dart candidates and may encode
one as SQL `NULL`. Without a Dart null candidate, nullable rows retain SQL's
three-valued behavior and match neither operator. Inputs are not deduplicated
or auto-chunked, so callers own every provider's parameter limit. Use the
explicit `updateAll` / `deleteAll` plus `allRows` path when all rows are
intended.

`insertOnConflictDoNothing` requires non-empty values and an explicit conflict
target. Every target column must belong to the table, and the column list must
match a conflict arbiter accepted by the selected database. Other index shapes
remain explicit `BoundSql`. SQLite, D1, and PostgreSQL compile the exact
target-aware operation. MySQL rejects it before reading either iterable or
touching the database; Odroe does not substitute `INSERT IGNORE` or a different
conflict contract.

`insertMany` compiles one multi-row `INSERT`. It requires at least one row and
at least one assignment per row. Every row must use the same table columns,
objects, and order; duplicate or foreign columns are rejected during preflight,
before SQL construction or I/O. It is not an `atomicWrite` batch of separate
statements. Odroe does not auto-chunk, so split large inputs according to the
selected provider's bound-parameter limit.

SQLite, D1, and PostgreSQL can append `RETURNING`; correlate the result through
a stable business key such as `id`, because the database does not promise input
order. MySQL/MariaDB executes the multi-row write with `.execute(database)` and
has no `RETURNING`; read back by stable keys when rows are needed.

`SqlQueries` preserves its selected dialect on every generated `BoundSql`.
SQLite and D1 accept `SqlDialect.sqlite`, PostgreSQL accepts
`SqlDialect.postgres`, and MySQL/MariaDB accepts `SqlDialect.mysql`. An explicit
mismatch throws `SqlException` with `SqlErrorCode.unsupported` before that
statement reaches the database.

## BoundSql escape hatch

Use `BoundSql.parts` for SQL outside the typed relational surface.

```dart
final statement = BoundSql.parts(
  <String>['SELECT "title" FROM "posts" WHERE "id" = ', ''],
  <SqlValue>[sqlInt.encode(42)],
  kind: SqlStatementKind.rowReturning,
  dialect: SqlDialect.postgres,
);
final titles = await database.query(
  statement,
  (row) => row.read(0, sqlText),
);
```

Fragments are trusted source. Values are bound. Drivers insert native
placeholders between fragments without parsing or rewriting their contents.
Handwritten `BoundSql.raw` and `BoundSql.parts` statements default to
`dialect: null`: no compatibility is claimed, so drivers continue to accept
them. This does not prove that the SQL is portable. Pin database-specific SQL
with `dialect:` to opt into the same preflight protection as typed queries.

Top-level calls may leave handwritten SQL as `unknown`, but a wrong terminal
may then be detected only after the database executes it. Declare the kind
when it is known. `atomicWrite` and transaction callbacks require an explicit
`write` or `rowReturning` kind, so transaction control and implicitly
committing DDL stay outside framework-managed transactions.

D1 additionally requires `rowReturning` on every `query` call because its raw
result cannot distinguish an empty query from a write. `SqlQueries` supplies
that marker automatically.

## Application ownership

`DatabaseModule` makes ownership explicit. An application-level Flutter
context may own a native database:

```dart
App(
  modules: <DatabaseModule>[
    DatabaseModule.owned(
      SqliteDatabase.open(appWritableDatabasePath),
    ),
  ],
  builder: (app) => Product(database: app.read(databaseKey)),
);
```

A native server instead creates one database or pool for the process and lets
each request borrow it:

```dart
final database = PostgresDatabase.pool(
  host: 'localhost',
  database: 'app',
  username: 'app',
  password: secret,
);

Server createServer() => generated.createServer(
  modules: () => <DatabaseModule>[DatabaseModule.borrowed(database)],
  onClose: database.close,
);
```

Handlers, loaders, and server functions read `databaseKey`. Request disposal
does not close the shared pool; `Server.close()` drains requests first and then
runs `onClose` once. Using `DatabaseModule.owned(database)` in
`Server.modules` is incorrect because modules are request-scoped. Edge D1
bindings have no reliable process shutdown event and remain invocation-owned;
the Fetch bootstrap does not manufacture an `onClose` callback.

`appWritableDatabasePath` above is an absolute writable path resolved by the
Flutter application for its platform; a relative process working directory is
not a portable mobile persistence location. The ownership boundary for every
driver is:

| Driver | Resource scope | Module and close contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SQLite in Flutter | Application | `DatabaseModule.owned`, closed with `AppContext` |
| SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB on a native server | Process | request `borrowed`, then `Server` `onClose` |
| Cloudflare D1 | Invocation | invocation `owned` wrapper; runtime still owns the binding |

The generated native bootstrap awaits either a synchronous or asynchronous
`createServer`, so MySQL can connect before the listener starts:

```dart
Future<Server> createServer() async {
  final database = await MysqlDatabase.open(
    host: 'localhost',
    database: 'app',
    username: 'app',
    password: secret,
  );
  return generated.createServer(
    modules: () => <DatabaseModule>[
      DatabaseModule.borrowed(database),
    ],
    onClose: database.close,
  );
}
```

In a Cloudflare-only server entry, wrap D1 once per invocation instead of
putting it in process modules:

```dart
@JS()
library;

import 'dart:js_interop';

import 'package:odroe/database_d1.dart';
import 'package:odroe/server_fetch.dart';

import 'routes.server.dart' as generated;

extension type WorkerEnvironment(JSObject _) implements JSObject {
  external JSObject get DB;
}

Server createServer() => generated.createServer(
  invocationModules: (invocation) {
    final bindings = invocation.requireBindings<FetchBindings>();
    final environment = WorkerEnvironment(bindings.raw);
    return <DatabaseModule>[
      DatabaseModule.owned(D1SqlDatabase.fromBinding(environment.DB)),
    ];
  },
);
```

## One query, two runtimes

The repository example makes `/posts/42` cross the complete product path:
Flutter page → Query → generated typed RPC → HTTP → Server → `DatabaseModule`
→ typed SQL. Its shared handler imports only `package:odroe/database.dart`:

```dart
final post = await postQueries
    .selectTable(
      posts,
      where: posts.id.equals(context.data),
    )
    .oneOrNull(context.request.read(databaseKey));
if (post == null) throw const NotFound('Post not found.');
return post.title;
```

`oneOrNull` accepts exactly zero or one decoded row. It returns `null` for no
row and rejects multiple rows instead of silently selecting one.

One conditional export keeps platform drivers out of the wrong artifact:

```dart
export 'server_cloudflare.dart'
    if (dart.library.io) 'server_native.dart'
    show createServer;
```

The native entry opens one process-owned file SQLite database, defaulting to
`.odroe/app.sqlite3`. Initialization completes before the listener can bind,
requests borrow it, and `Server.close()` closes it. `ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` may
override the file; production should use an absolute path on writable persistent
storage. Before opening the listener, the entry loads `migrations/*.sql` and
calls `SqliteDatabase.applyMigrations`. `ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH` may override
that directory. Both default relative paths resolve from the server process's
working directory.
During native prerender the CLI overrides `ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` with a temporary
database. `--sqlite-migrations` pins the selected project source, and the CLI
deletes the database afterward, keeping builds independent of local state.

The Cloudflare entry creates an invocation-owned D1 wrapper;
Wrangler applies the same top-level SQL history instead of running DDL in the
Fetch bootstrap. Shared source does not mean shared state: Native records exact
source in `_odroe_migrations`, while Wrangler owns `d1_migrations` in D1.

The example owns a locked local Wrangler toolchain. With Node 22+ and npm 10.9+,
its D1 path is:

```sh
flutter pub get
npm ci
dart run odroe build --no-server --sqlite-migrations migrations web
npm run cloudflare:migrate:local
npm run cloudflare:dev
```

`cloudflare:dev` compiles the current Dart Worker before it starts local
Wrangler and watches later server changes. Initial route generation and compile
must succeed; after startup, a failed generation or compile leaves the previous
Worker live. The command never applies migrations or touches remote D1.
Flutter Web and static assets remain an explicit build step.

Native and local D1 both return `Odroe post 42`. A missing ID
returns a typed RPC 404. Applying a remote migration or deploying the Worker is
a separate state-changing operation.

## PostgreSQL connections

Keep `PostgresDatabase.open(...)` when one serialized connection is the right
boundary. Server workloads can choose a lazy pool instead:

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database_postgres.dart';

final database = PostgresDatabase.pool(
  host: 'localhost',
  database: 'app',
  username: 'app',
  password: secret,
  settings: const PoolSettings(maxConnectionCount: 4),
);
```

The default `pool(...)` settings use
`PoolSettings(maxConnectionCount: 4)`. Treat that limit as a database-cost
control and size it for the deployment. Set it explicitly when passing custom
`PoolSettings`; the underlying default for a null limit is one connection.
`poolUrl(...)` has the same one-connection default unless the URL sets
`max_connection_count`. Construction does not open a connection; the first
operation leases one.

Top-level operations may run concurrently. Each `transaction` and
`atomicWrite` operation leases one connection for its complete lifetime.
Concurrency remains bounded by the configured connection limit. Separate
top-level calls are not guaranteed to reuse one session. Temporary tables,
`SET`, and other session-local work are outside the Odroe pool contract. Use
single-connection `open(...)` when that state must span statements, or complete
the whole unit directly inside a caller-owned `Pool.run(...)` callback.

`PostgresDatabase.fromPool(pool)` borrows the pool by default. Closing the
database leaves that pool open. Pass `ownsPool: true` when the database should
close it. Pools created by `pool(...)` and `poolUrl(...)` are owned and released
by `close()`. `Connection`, `ConnectionSettings`, `Pool`, and `PoolSettings`
are all exported from `package:odroe/database_postgres.dart`. Applications only
need a direct `package:postgres` dependency for advanced APIs that are not part
of Odroe's public constructor signatures.

## MySQL and MariaDB connections

`MysqlDatabase.open(...)` owns one eager, serialized connection. It is the
lowest-cost choice for a small process or a workload that needs one stable
session. A concurrent server can instead create an owned lazy pool:

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database_mysql.dart';

final database = MysqlDatabase.pool(
  host: 'localhost',
  database: 'app',
  username: 'app',
  password: secret,
  maxConnections: 4,
  maxPendingOperations: 32,
  queueTimeout: Duration(seconds: 10),
);
```

The defaults allow four physical connections and 32 operations waiting for
capacity. Treat `maxConnections` as the concurrency and database-cost ceiling;
`maxPendingOperations` bounds in-process memory and shutdown backlog. Once the
queue is full, another operation fails immediately with
`SqlErrorCode.unavailable`. `queueTimeout` defaults to ten seconds and covers
only time still waiting for capacity. After an opening starts, `connectTimeout`
bounds its handshake and UTC session setup. Construction itself opens nothing.
`transaction` and `atomicWrite` keep one connection for their complete
lifetime. The pool does not retry operations, so a failed write is never
silently replayed.

`close()` first drains every operation accepted before closing, including pool
waiters, and then closes all owned connections. A failed connection open frees
capacity and advances the queue. If transaction start or rollback leaves the
session state uncertain, Odroe destroys that physical connection instead of
returning it to another request.

Separate top-level calls can use different sessions. Do not send manual
transaction control or rely on temporary tables and session-level `SET` across
calls. Use `transaction(...)` for a multi-statement unit, or use `open(...)`
when session identity itself is the application requirement.

## Runtime matrix

| Entrypoint | Runtime | Status | Current boundary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `database_sqlite.dart` | Native SQLite | Verified | Real CRUD, `RETURNING`, batches, transactions, and append-only migrations |
| `database_postgres.dart` | Dart VM + PostgreSQL | Verified | Real single-connection, pooled concurrency, and transaction suite |
| `database_d1.dart` | Cloudflare D1 | Preview | Local Wrangler/Workerd contract; atomic batches, no interactive transaction |
| `database_mysql.dart` | Dart VM + MySQL/MariaDB | Preview | Single connection: MySQL 8.4 + MariaDB 11.8; bounded pool: MariaDB 11.8 |
| RPC | Flutter Web | Available | Calls a server-owned database; no browser SQL driver |

The MySQL/MariaDB driver does not support nested transactions, multiple result
sets, portable `TIME` decoding, or `RETURNING` through `SqlDialect.mysql`.

MySQL statements without bound values reject `;` before I/O so the driver's
text protocol cannot execute multiple statements. Omit trailing semicolons.
Statements with bound values use the prepared protocol.

D1 maps Cloudflare's documented network loss, storage reset, code-update reset,
and transient remote-node errors to `SqlErrorCode.unavailable`. Capacity,
overload, resource-limit, and large-write timeout errors remain `driver` because
they require application or schema changes rather than blind retries. The Odroe
adapter adds no retries; the Cloudflare runtime may still retry read-only
queries under its own contract. An application may add a retry only when its
operation is idempotent. See Cloudflare's [retry guidance](https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/best-practices/retry-queries/)
for the provider signals and backoff requirements. D1 has not been verified in a
remote Cloudflare deployment.

All drivers live in the same Dart package. Entrypoints keep driver APIs and
reachable Dart code separate, but pub still resolves the shared dependency
graph. With SQLite's default configuration, its Native build hook contributes a
dynamic library even when an application does not import that driver; Dart tree
shaking does not undo dependency resolution or build hooks. Odroe therefore
uses [`dart build cli`](https://dart.dev/tools/dart-build) and publishes the
complete Native bundle instead of only its executable. Inspect each target's
real artifact when binary size matters.

## Schema and migrations

Native SQLite and D1 can consume one application-owned, append-only SQL history:

```text
migrations/
├── 0001_posts.sql
└── 0002_unify_posts.sql
```

Names must match `NNNN_snake_case.sql`; versions are unique and gaps are
allowed. Always pass the complete history. Once a file has been applied, never
edit, delete, or rename it, and never insert a lower version below an applied
higher one. Append a new file instead.

```dart
final migrations = readSqliteMigrations(migrationsPath);
final applied = await database.applyMigrations(migrations);
```

The native runner returns the number applied by this call. It serializes with
normal driver work, takes a cross-connection write lock with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`,
uses SQLite's parser to execute every statement without naïvely splitting on
semicolons, and records the complete file in the same transaction. Connections
wait briefly for a competing SQLite writer. After taking that lock, the runner
revalidates the complete source and applied history so two processes cannot
accept incompatible histories. A failed current file rolls back while earlier
files remain applied. Its reserved `main._odroe_migrations` ledger stores the
exact SQL string; the runner verifies its canonical schema and rejects direct
or trigger-based changes without a hash dependency.

Wrangler reads the same files for D1 and maintains its own ledger. Run it as an
explicit deployment step; do not apply migrations during a Fetch invocation.
Wrangler records names rather than source, so immutable artifacts and review are
still required to catch D1 history edits. Keep scripts inside the SQLite/D1
common subset. Omit transaction and connection controls: `BEGIN`, `COMMIT`,
`END`, `ROLLBACK`, `SAVEPOINT`, `RELEASE`, `ATTACH`, `DETACH`, and `VACUUM`.
`writable_schema` and the reserved ledger name are also rejected.

This is a small application-SQL runner, not a cross-provider migration
framework. PostgreSQL and MySQL/MariaDB continue to use an application-owned
provider path; Odroe does not generate schema diffs, entities, or down
migrations.
